{"version":"2026.10.11-r25","id":"Q3194310","formula":"C₁₄H₁₄O₃","hill":"C14H14O3","elements":["H","C","O"],"composition":{"C":14.0,"H":14.0,"O":3.0},"names":{"en":"Kavain"},"descriptions":{"en":"Kavain is the principal kavalactone found in the roots of the kava plant (Piper methysticum), where it contributes significantly to the plant's psychoactive and anxiolytic effects. Kavain exhibits anticonvulsant properties by modulating voltage-dependent sodium and calcium channels, and it may influence mood and anxiety through reversible inhibition of monoamine oxidase A and monoamine oxidase B, potentially affecting serotonin, dopamine, and norepinephrine signaling. Although it does not bind to the benzodiazepine site of GABAA receptors, kavain potentiates GABA activity at extrasynaptic α4β2δ GABAA receptors and overlaps with the modulatory pathways of certain general anesthetics."},"fame":12,"pubchemCid":5281565,"mass":230.26,"charge":0,"smiles":"COC1=CC(=O)O[C@H](C1)/C=C/C2=CC=CC=C2","inchikey":"XEAQIWGXBXCYFX-GUOLPTJISA-N","iupac":"(2R)-4-methoxy-2-[(E)-2-phenylethenyl]-2,3-dihydropyran-6-one","classes":[{"qid":"Q113145171","names":{"en":"type of chemical entity","ru":"тип химического объекта","uk":"тип хімічної сутності"}}],"structureInfo":{"kind":"molecule","source":"pubchem","ref":"5281565","atoms":31,"license":"PubChem (NIH), public data"},"structure":{"kind":"molecule","atoms":[{"x":-1.584,"y":-1.409,"z":-0.077,"el":"O"},{"x":-4.134,"y":1.846,"z":0.3,"el":"O"},{"x":-3.387,"y":-2.784,"z":-0.034,"el":"O"},{"x":-1.039,"y":-0.146,"z":0.361,"el":"C"},{"x":-1.874,"y":1.012,"z":-0.173,"el":"C"},{"x":-3.31,"y":0.751,"z":0.146,"el":"C"},{"x":0.376,"y":-0.075,"z":-0.11,"el":"C"},{"x":-3.785,"y":-0.49,"z":0.294,"el":"C"},{"x":1.429,"y":-0.027,"z":0.72,"el":"C"},{"x":-2.914,"y":-1.656,"z":0.06,"el":"C"},{"x":2.838,"y":0.053,"z":0.301,"el":"C"},{"x":3.187,"y":-0.329,"z":-0.985,"el":"C"},{"x":3.79,"y":0.51,"z":1.199,"el":"C"},{"x":-4.73,"y":2.206,"z":-0.932,"el":"C"},{"x":4.522